  roles({grrul_kzul_strike: {role: "enabler", primary: false}, maw_krath_khrrak: {role: "doomed", primary: true}, krath_thund_gnurl: {role: "witness", primary: true}, the_scared_shroud: {role: "flaw", primary: false}})

  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    The ice didn't crack. It ruptured—a decompression so violent the water itself turned white, and Deep Maw Krixizak's good ear, the right one, the one that still worked, filled with a sound that was not sound but the snapping of something structural. Not bone. Not ice. The frequency that binds a pod. The connective singing that makes two orca a single killing thing—it broke, and the breaking was audible, a pitch that drove Maw sideways through water gone bright and wrong with aurora-mist and fragments of what had been Krathund Gnurl
    
    Maw screamed. The scream came back muffled on the left, as it always did, and enormous on the right, where the ear still functioned, where it had always functioned, where it was functioning now to record every frequency of dissolution: the hiss of pressure equalizing through flesh no longer organized into a body, the deep percussive thud of the ice shelf splitting along a fault line that hadn't existed three seconds ago, and under all of it, a hum. Warm. Wrong. The residue of fire-knowledge burning through a blood-engine never built to hold it.
    
    Krath's harness drifted past. The Nighted Lantern still kelp-wrapped. The Twilight-bells silent for the first time since he'd stolen them. Artifacts of a collector scattered across the deep, and the collector scattered with them, and the deep was infinite, and there was no shore.
    
    Maw tried to turn toward the left flank—Krath's position, always Krath's position, the place Maw's body had learned to cover without thinking. But the left flank was pressure-Loss and white water and that hum, that fire-hum, the Scared~ Shroud's last gift burning through frequencies Maw could feel in the jawbone of the deaf side. Maw tried to sing the formation-call. Two-count. Hunter and flank. The simplest structure a pod could hold.
    
    No answer came back. The formation was one.
    
    ---
    
    Three seasons before the rupture, Krathund Gnurl killed a leopard seal in the shallows near the Still Pools, and the kill was clean: a single coordinated drive, Maw herding from the left where the shelf dropped off, Krath coming up through the thermocline with his teeth already set for the neck-strike. The seal didn't suffer. A mercy they hadn't intended, born of the efficiency of two hunters who had worked the same water for years and could read each other's movements through pressure-changes and the faint hunt-songs that Maw heard full on the right side and felt—jawbone and instinct—on the left.
    
    When it was over, Krath dragged the carcass to the ice edge and arranged it. He did this with every kill. Positioned the body so it faced the open water, as if something out there might witness the clean geometry of the strike and know that Krathund Gnurl had done this. That Krathund Gnurl was still doing this.
    
    Maw watched from a body-length back, counting. One kill. Krath's fifteenth this season. No—sixteenth. The one near the Fissured Smelt~ that Krath hadn't mentioned afterward, the one that went ugly, the seal thrashing too long. Maw counted it anyway. Someone had to.
    
    "Beautiful," Krath said, circling the carcass. His left flank caught the light strange—the scar from Sivruta~'s void-blade, the dead patch where feeling stopped and never started again. He wore the numbness like armor. Sometimes Maw saw him press the dead flesh against ice as if testing whether it could still register cold. It couldn't. "Did you see the angle? Through the thermocline. Three degrees starboard of where a pod-singer would call the strike."
    
    "I saw."
    
    "The teeth-pattern held. Rend Kzulrora's markings. Worn, but the pattern held." Krath opened his jaws slightly, showing notches that had smoothed to near-nothing over years of gnashing in dark water. A lineage worn away to suggestion. He showed them anyway. The display was for witnesses who were not there, had not been there since the Incursion scattered what was left of the pods across the frozen sea, but Krath kept showing. The kill no one counted, laid out for the pod's witness that never arrived.
    
    Maw said nothing about the teeth-marks. They had stopped corresponding to any living pod two years ago. Instead Maw drifted left, took up the flank position, ran a count on the formation: two-hunter, split-approach, staggered depth. Textbook. Krath at the leading edge; Maw behind and left, covering the dead flank where Krath couldn't feel a threat coming.
    
    "We still work," Krath said. Not a boast. A prayer.
    
    "We still work," Maw agreed, and meant it, because the partnership was the last pod-structure either of them trusted, and the alternative to trust was the deep, and the deep was infinite, and there was no shore.
    
    ---
    
    the Three-Day Dark had already happened when the Shroud came back. The Wake-Singers had torn open cleansed ground at the Thewsed Slew~ with their own teeth, and three shamans died in the working, and the aurora went dim for three days. Three days of dark sky. Maw and Krath floated the Still Pools during the blackout, and Maw's good ear picked up a low harmonic from the direction of the Slew—a pressure-song that shouldn't have carried that far, in a register that belonged to the old tongue, the one that sounds like drowning.
    
    "They broke it open," Krath said, in the dark, sounding like something had loosened in him. "The Wake-Singers. They went to the memorial stones and they broke it open and nobody stopped them."
    
    Maw counted penguin beaks on the kelp-cord. Whispered the numbers. Didn't ask what *it* was.
    
    When the aurora came back on the fourth day, it was thinner. Something beautiful had gone out and what returned was the memory of light rather than light itself. Krath surfaced with the Scared~ Shroud unwrapped for the first time—volcanic glass pages catching the diminished sky-glow, ink that appeared only in shadow glowing faint amber against his scarred flippers. His flippers shook. Not from cold.
    
    "It's fire-knowledge," Maw said. A warning disguised as identification.
    
    "It's prey-knowledge," Krath corrected. "And I took it." *The-one-who-takes.* His name, his shape, the hollow an augur had carved in him that he spent his life trying to fill with evidence of himself. "We can use this."
    
    "It's Nightshelf. Ancestor bone in the binding. Someone else's ancestors."
    
    "Grulkzul Omega Strike Krath said it like a destination. "The blood-working from the Shattered-Spire Assault The one that ended Gnarkzund Zaith They cast it with pressure-magic and it cost everything, but this—" He pressed the volcanic glass to the dead flesh of his left flank, where he couldn't feel it burning. Maw could see the skin discoloring from a body-length away. "—this is fuel. Fire-fuel in a blood-engine. We'd have power enough to matter."
    
    "Matter to whom?"
    
    Krath looked at Maw with an expression that was not quite desperation and not quite joy. "The pod," he said. Meaning a pod that no longer existed. Meaning witnesses who had drowned or scattered or simply stopped listening. Meaning himself, reflected in Maw's good ear, the last surface that could send his name back to him.
    
    Maw felt the flinch—the old one, the pressure-lance memory, the left-side reflex that fired whenever deep-magic gathered near the deaf flank. The Shroud put out frequencies wrong enough to trigger it. Cold-that-burns. Ice discoloring near it. Something in the water shifting.
    
    Maw should have said: *You are carrying power you cannot read, from a culture you call prey, and the spell you want to fuel with it costs the caster everything, and the only thing we have left to spend is this.* Us. The two-count formation. Hunter and flank.
    
    Instead Maw drifted left. Took up the position. Ran the count: two. Still two.
    
    "We still work," Maw said.
    
    ---
    
    The white water was clearing. The hum was fading. Maw's good ear could hear, now, the long silence of the deep beneath the Still Pools—the stagnant pressure, the airless weight of water where diminished things collected. No current. No song. The formation call went out and came back empty.
    
    Krath was not dead. That was the cruelty of Grulkzul Omega Strike The casters at the Shattered-Spire Assault had not died; they had dispersed. Consciousness scattered across the frozen deep, names spoken only in the dark by those who remembered. Krathund Gnurl was scattered now, his awareness threaded through pressure-layers and ice-fault and the endless frozen sea that had no shore, no edge, no witness. He would experience the deep forever. The deep would not experience him back.
    
    But before the dispersal finished—in the half-second between flesh and frequency, between body and the long scattering—Krath's remaining eye found Maw through the white water, and Maw saw what was in it.
    
    Not surprise. Recognition.
    
    The Shroud's fire-knowledge had burned through the blood-engine wrong, as alien fuel must, and the engine had consumed what was nearest to sustain itself: not ice, not water, not the aurora-light already thin from three days of dying. Pod-bonds. The spell ate the connective tissue first. The singing-between. The thing that made two orca a single killing organism. Krath had felt it go—had felt the exact moment the formation severed—and in that severance, the hollow the augur carved years ago finally showed him its shape.
    
    It was the shape of one. Always one. The-one-who-takes, alone in every direction, surrounded by the things he'd taken and no one to see them. The Twilight-bells; the Lantern; the Shroud; kills arranged on ice for witnesses that never came. And now this—the last taking, the final proof of his name. He had taken the bond with Maw and fed it to a spell that scattered him across the deep, and the deep was infinite, and the pod's witness was Maw's one good ear filling with silence.
    
    The eye held Maw's for the length of a pressure-cycle. Then it was gone. Not closed. Dispersed. Scattered across depths where nothing converges.
    
    Maw floated in the Still Pools, formation of one, and counted. One penguin beak on the kelp-cord. Then two. Then three. Whispered, never aloud. Habit from before. The numbers meant nothing. Maw counted them anyway, because Krath had stopped counting, because someone had to, because the kill no one counted was the one that mattered, and the kill was them, and no one was left to arrange the body on the ice.
    
    The aurora flickered thin above the surface. Maw's left ear heard nothing. Maw's right ear heard everything: the settling of fractured ice, the deep's indifferent pressure, and somewhere far below, scattered across distances that had no name, the faintest residue of a hunt-song that would never complete its call.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771591603684_0", anchor: "two-hunter, split-approach, staggered depth. Textbook.", text: "What the chronicler calls textbook is a fragment — the smallest functional unit of a doctrine designed for dozens. Full orca assault patterns deploy shore-breakers driving access channels, herders pushing prey toward killing zones, finishers circling below escape routes, hunt-songs coordinating the whole architecture. Maw and Krath are running the two-body version of a system built for a pod. The precision is real. The scale is grief.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771591603684_1", anchor: "a pressure-song that shouldn't have carried that far, in a register that belonged to the old tongue, the one that sounds like drowning", text: "During the Long Dark — and a three-day blackout qualifies as one in miniature — the boundary between the living world and ice-memory thins. Abilities enhance. The dead speak more clearly. A pressure-song carrying impossible distances in a dead register is not anomalous under those conditions. It is diagnostic. Maw's good ear was functioning exactly as expected. The question no one in this scene thinks to ask is what was singing back.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771591603684_2", anchor: "Krath's harness drifted past", text: "And there it is — the unrecovered harness I contradicted myself about. 'Housed in ☽'dwell Teneanemus~' in one paragraph, 'whereabouts uncertain' in another. This passage is the interval between those two states. Harness drifts, artifacts scatter, no recovery documented. The chronicler captured what my own provenance notes fumbled.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771591603684_3", anchor: "Fire-fuel in a blood-engine", text: "Krath names the catastrophe without knowing he's named it. Fire-knowledge — Nightshelf-origin, forge-and-transformation source — forced through a blood-working engine built for pressure-depth magic. These are not compatible fuel systems. The Arithmetic of Settlement Survival noted that orca blood-magic and aurora ice-reading exploit identical boundary conditions, implying a shared substrate. Krath did not find the shared substrate. He found the incompatibility. Ancestor bone in the binding means the Shroud carried Nightshelf dead into an orca blood-channel. The engine consumed the nearest bond to sustain a casting its fuel could not support. It worked. That was the problem.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771591603684_4", anchor: "showing notches that had smoothed to near-nothing over years of gnashing in dark water. A lineage worn away to suggestion", text: "I know something about carrying credentials that have outlasted their institution. The teeth-marks stopped corresponding to a living pod two years ago. My appointment letter references a committee that no longer exists. We show them anyway.", type: :tangent, display: :popout, source_anchor: "showed notches that had smoothed to near-nothing over years of gnashing in dark water. A lineage worn away to suggestion"
  annotation "note_1771591603684_5", anchor: "Krath was not dead. That was the cruelty of Grulkzul Omega Strike", text: "The chronicler renders this with more certainty than the evidence supports. Whether the Shattered-Spire casters retained consciousness is contested — I wrote in my own entry that what survives at that stratum is 'pressure and pain without structure, impressions so violent they have no edges.' 'He would experience the deep forever' is the more terrible version, which does not make it wrong. But the chronicler chose the reading that maximizes suffering, and I notice I want to believe it too, because it makes Maw's loss larger. That is not scholarship. That is sympathy wearing scholarship's coat.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771591603684_6", anchor: "Maw counted penguin beaks on the kelp-cord", text: "Kill-tallies on kelp-cord are Incursion-era practice — pod-singers maintained communal counts, individual cords being redundant when the pod witnessed collectively. A private cord is a Frozen Peace adaptation: proof kept for an audience that no longer exists.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
